{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And Jacob went out.</b> Rashi's comment: because of the evil of the daughters of Canaan, etc. — the explanation of his explanation is this: this is what it says — because he interrupted the matter of Esau, the pericope of Jacob — as it is written, 'And Esau saw that Isaac blessed,' etc. And why did he interrupt? The answer to the matter: because of the evil of the daughters of Canaan in the eyes of Isaac, Esau went to Ishmael, and once the matter of Esau is completed, it returns to its first matter, which is the matter of Jacob. And why is this pericope closed (setumah)? Because he was hidden in the house of Eber. Another interpretation: because he concealed his departure from the city on account of Esau his brother, the pericope was closed. Also, in the entire sedar you will not find any pericope break, unlike in all the other sedarim. And furthermore, the matter is connected above to 'And Isaac sent Jacob and he went to Paddan-aram,' as Rashi explained, except that he interrupted in between with 'And Esau saw that Isaac blessed Jacob,' etc., and it returns and explains his departure on account of the dream of the ladder and his going to Paddan-aram." "<b>Be'er-sheva.</b> A special place for oath-taking, and he went out from there lest they make him swear that he did not deceive regarding the blessings. <b>Be'er-sheva.</b> It was a place close to Hebron, as it is written in the Akedah: 'And Abraham dwelt in Be'er-sheva,' and adjacent to it: 'And Sarah died in Kiryat-arba, which is Hebron.' And furthermore, it is written here <b>And Jacob went out from Be'er-sheva</b>, and it is written in the pericope of Vayishlah: 'And Jacob came to Isaac his father at Mamre, Kiryat-arba, which is Hebron.'" "<b>And he went toward Haran.</b> On that very day he went as far as Haran, and he set out from Kiryat-arba, as we say in the Gemara. And the Sages said: from Kiryat-arba to Haran is a journey of seventeen days, and Jacob walked it in twelve hours." "<b>Toward Haran.</b> It is Haran; it is Paddan-aram, as it is written above: 'to Laban the brother of Haran,' and it is written in the Gemara: 'Arise, go to Paddan-aram,' etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:06:31.353672824Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}